course: dictionary
lesson: 8864
title: Words for people who buy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A shopper is a person who buys things in a shop, at a market, or online. The word comes from shop, with -er added to show the person who does the action. Shoppers is the plural: more than one person.
explain: Context helps you pin down the meaning. «Packaging is designed to sell, so smart shoppers read the label, not the box.» The word smart tells you a shopper can be clever about spending, not just someone who buys. Try it: swap in "person who buys things" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Writers sometimes compare one thing to another to make it easy to picture. «A honeybee... flies off with yellow balls of pollen packed on its back legs, like a shopper with two full bags.» The bee is not a shopper, but it carries its load like one.
fact: A shopper is a person who is buying things in a shop, at a market, or online.
fact: A smart shopper compares prices to spend money wisely.
fact: A window shopper looks at things in shop windows but does not buy them.
fact: «Busy shoppers» means many people buying things at the same time.
fact: Shopper is a noun. One person is a shopper, and more than one person is shoppers.
exercise: «Smart shoppers read the label, not the box.» What is a smart shopper? | Someone who only looks at boxes | Someone who never buys anything | *Someone who compares prices to spend money wisely | A person who works in a shop
exercise: Which word means a person who looks in shop windows but does not buy? | honeybee | tariff | busy shopper | *window shopper
exercise: What is the plural of shopper? | shops | shopped | shopping | *shoppers
test: In the honeybee sentence, what is the bee compared to? | A shop window | *A shopper with two full bags | A tax on goods | A flower with yellow petals
test: What does a shopper do? | Carries pollen on its back legs | Sells goods in a shop | *Buys things in a shop, at a market, or online | Makes tariffs on goods from abroad
exam: «Just then the shopper is back, staggering under a full basket.» What is the shopper doing? | Looking at prices only | Flying from flower to flower | *Coming back with a full basket of things she has bought | Making a tax on goods from abroad
